\begin{abstract}
We present observations of the \(K\)-doublet transitions of formaldehyde, \(\mathrm{H_2CO}\), toward the L1204 molecular cloud complex. The \(1_{10}\!-\!1_{11}\) and \(2_{11}\!-\!2_{12}\) lines are detected in absorption against the cosmic microwave background over an extended region, demonstrating that the anomalous excitation of \(\mathrm{H_2CO}\) is not confined to compact continuum sources or active star-forming cores. Radiative-transfer modeling of the line ratios indicates widespread gas with intermediate densities, \(n(\mathrm{H_2})\sim10^{3.5}\!-\!10^{5}\,\mathrm{cm^{-3}}\), while complementary temperature constraints imply that much of this material is warmer than typical quiescent dark-cloud gas. The absorption morphology and velocity structure broadly follow the molecular cloud, but reveal a component that is less centrally concentrated than conventional high-density tracers. These results show that warm, moderately dense molecular gas constitutes a substantial and spatially extended phase of L1204. Because the background illumination is supplied by the nearly uniform cosmic microwave background, \(\mathrm{H_2CO}\) centimeter-wave absorption provides a sensitive, distance-independent probe of otherwise difficult-to-detect molecular material and offers a direct means of mapping the density structure surrounding embedded star formation.
\end{abstract}
